Subject: wasix: check for signals and exit without locking on the fast path

Every WASIX syscall begins with do_pending_operations, which drains the
thread's signal queue and then asks the thread and the process whether
they have finished. Draining the queue locks its mutex and swaps a
vector out, once when no signal handler is installed and a second time
in process_signals when one is; asking about the finished status borrows
a tokio watch and clones the status out of it, once for the thread and
once for the process. A guest that has no signal queued and is not
exiting pays all of that on every call. In a profile of a guest under
load, those checks were 7% of the runtime's user-mode samples, which is
more than the clock syscall many of them precede.

Give each of the two questions an atomic that answers it in the common
case. WasiThread gains a signals_pending flag, set by signal() while it
holds the queue's lock and cleared by the two pops before they take the
queue, also under that lock; pop_signals answers with an empty vector
and no lock when the flag is clear. Because the flag only ever changes
with the lock held, a push that races a pop sets it again after the pop
cleared it, so no signal is stranded, and a read that finds it clear
has not passed a queued signal. The queue is still reachable through
signals(), which callers use to read it; queueing a signal there rather
than through signal() would now be invisible, so the accessor says so.

OwnedTaskStatus gains a finished flag, set before the finished status
is sent to the watch and never cleared, since a task that has finished
stays finished; try_finished consults it before borrowing the watch.
Both the thread's and the process's try_join go through that status, so
the pair of borrows the entry path makes becomes a pair of atomic
loads. A reader that finds the flag set in the instant before the
status is sent falls through to the watch and reads Running, exactly as
a read an instant earlier would have, and sees the exit on its next
check, which every syscall makes.

The slow paths are untouched: once either flag says yes, the same lock,
the same swap and the same watch borrow run as they did. Unit tests
cover a signal queued after a pop being seen by the next pop, the
subscribing pop clearing the flag as well, and a thread and a task
status being seen as finished as soon as they are.

